 "amendments": {
  "items": {
   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the real property tax law, in relation to granting a real property tax exemption to nonprofit organizations that purchase real property after the taxable status date",
    "basePrintNo": "S2273",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S2273-2017",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [
      {
       "alternate": false,
       "chamber": "SENATE",
       "districtCode": 11,
       "fullName": "Tony Avella",
       "imgName": "391_tony_avella.jpg",
       "incumbent": false,
       "memberId": 391,
       "sessionMemberId": 920,
       "sessionYear": 2017,
       "shortName": "AVELLA"
      },
      {
       "alternate": false,
       "chamber": "SENATE",
       "districtCode": 43,
       "fullName": "Kathleen A. Marchione",
       "imgName": "435_kathleen_a._marchione.jpg",
       "incumbent": false,
       "memberId": 435,
       "sessionMemberId": 940,
       "sessionYear": 2017,
       "shortName": "MARCHIONE"
      }
     ],
     "size": 2
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §§420-a & 420-b, RPT L",
    "lawSection": "Real Property Tax Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S2273\n\n TITLE OF BILL :  An act to amend the real property tax law, in\nrelation to granting a real property tax exemption to nonprofit\norganizations that purchase real property after the taxable status\ndate\n\n PURPOSE :\n\nTo authorize local municipal corporations (after local law adopted) to\naccept and administer nonprofit tax exemption applications after the\ntaxable status date under certain circumstances.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\n\nSections one and two of this bill amend Real Property Tax Law §§ 420-a\nand 420-b, respectively, to allow, at the option of the locality, that\nwhen a not-for-profit organization purchases property after the\ntaxable status date, it will be permitted to submit an application for\na real property tax exemption to the assessor. They also establish a\nprocedure to change the assessment roll and allows for the exemption\nbenefits granted upon purchase of property after the taxable status\ndate to be applied against the eligible individual's tax levy of the\nsubsequent year. If the municipal corporation has received\nreimbursement for the tax credit owed to the not-for-profit\norganization, the tax credit shall be paid to the municipal\ncorporation making the tax payment.\n\nSection three provides that the act shall take effect 90 days after\nits enactment.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nCurrently, nonprofit organizations that are eligible for a real\nproperty tax exemption do not receive it immediately, after the\npurchase of property. They must pay the full amount of taxes, in some\ncases up to almost two years after purchasing the property and seek\nstate approval to file for the exemption retroactively. Since the\nstate has deemed it proper to immediately tax property sold by someone\nreceiving a real property tax exemption to a purchaser not entitled to\nan exemption, it is equally proper to grant immediate exempt status to\nproperty purchased by a not-for-profit organization eligible for real\nproperty tax exemption.\n\n LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\n2015-2016: S.605 - Passed Senate, died in Assembly (both years)\n\n2014: S.2939\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\n\nNone to the state.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nNinetieth day succeeding enactment.\n",
